<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<StyleSet xmlns="http://adamantium/ui"
          xmlns:x="http://adamantium/ui/xaml/extensions">

    <!-- Docking - the furniture an editor actually IS. Style matching is by EXACT type here, so a derived control
         inherits nothing by default: that is why each of these says BasedOn explicitly instead of quietly relying on its
         base. A pane group IS a tab control and a pane IS a tab item, so they take those looks whole; the splitter needs
         its own, because a Thumb has no look at all - and a control with no template renders nothing and cannot be hit,
         which is exactly what makes a grip that refuses to be dragged.

         Everything about EDGES, STATES, folding and flyout placement below is behaviour rather than looks, and is
         carried over unchanged. Two things are this theme's own, and both come from the same place:

         THE TOOL CAPTION IS NOT ACCENT-FILLED. Fluent paints it with the accent, which reads well when one tool panel is
         open. An editor has six - Solution, Properties, Output, Errors, Watch, Find - and the accent stops meaning "this
         one" the moment more than one thing on screen wears it. Here the caption is a quiet band closed with a hairline,
         and WHICH panel you are in is said by the accent on its border, which is a mark exactly one panel carries at a
         time. Fluent has that border rule too; without the accent captions competing with it, it can finally be read.

         HOVER IS NEUTRAL, DRAGGING IS ACCENT. On the splitter Fluent uses the accent for both. Moving the pointer across
         a docked layout crosses splitters constantly and means nothing, so an ambient accent flash is the same spend as
         the captions. The drag is a deliberate act, one at a time - that one keeps the accent. -->

    <!-- A floating panel's window looks like any other window. Stated explicitly because matching here is by EXACT type:
         without this line a DockingWindow gets no template at all - and a window with no template is a frame with
         nothing in it, not a window that merely looks plain. -->
    <Style Selector="DockingWindow" BasedOn="Window"/>

    <!-- A hair of space between the panels and the window's own frame. MEASURED: with none, a panel's border sits in the
         same pixel as the window border - at 150% its 1.5 physical pixels straddle the seam, half of it under the frame
         and the rest smeared by antialiasing, so the accent below was visible only along the edge that touched nothing.
         One DIP is enough to let every side of every panel be seen. -->
    <Style Selector="DockingArea">
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{ResourceReference DockAreaMargin}"/>
        <!-- The ground BEHIND the panels, and it is darker than they are: what shows through between two of them is the
             groove that separates them. Nothing else draws that gap, so without this the panels' own grounds meet and
             the layout has no seams at all. -->
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference SolidBackgroundFillColorSecondary}"/>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="PaneGroup" BasedOn="TabControl"/>

    <!-- A panel is separated by its GROUND, not by a frame of its own. It first shipped with a hairline border on every
         group, and that is what a docked layout cannot afford: panels sit edge to edge, so two neighbours put two
         hairlines side by side and they read as one thick smeared line - and at fractional scaling they merge outright.
         Everything that has to live at a panel's edge then collides with it: the tab strip's accent indicator runs into
         it, and the header's ground covers it along the left.
         So the panel carries the LIGHTER ground and the docking area behind it the darker one; the gap between two
         panels is then a groove rather than a pair of lines. This is what Visual Studio does, and it is why its dense
         layouts stay readable at any number of splits. -->
    <Style Selector="PaneGroup">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}"/>
        <!-- The frame is always THERE and almost always INVISIBLE: constant thickness, transparent while the panel is
             not the one being worked in. Constant, because a border that appears on focus insets the panel's content by
             its own width and every zone switch would nudge the text. Transparent, because only one panel is active at
             a time - so exactly one frame is ever drawn and two neighbours can no longer put two hairlines side by
             side. Which is what a frame per panel did, and what merged them into one thick line. -->
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{ResourceReference StrokeThin}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- ...and the one that IS being worked in. This has to be the group's own frame rather than anything in a caption:
         a DOCUMENT group has no caption at all, and there can be many of them. -->
    <Style Selector="PaneGroup">
        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsActive" Value="true">
            <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefault}"/>
        </PropertyTrigger>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Pane" BasedOn="TabItem"/>

    <!-- A tab whose panel has folded down against a SIDE: the label lies on its side, because the strip that is left is
         a narrow column. BOTH sides turn the same way - top to bottom - so a glance down one edge reads the same as a
         glance down the other. Facing the text out of its own panel sounded symmetrical and reads as two different
         conventions in one window.
         Two styles with a literal angle each rather than one with a bound angle - a Transform is not a component and
         cannot be a binding target at all. -->
    <Style Selector="Pane[LabelRotation=Left]">
        <Setter Property="HeaderTemplate">
            <Setter.Value>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <!-- LAYOUT transform, not render: the strip has to be as WIDE as the turned text is tall. A render
                         transform would leave it measured for text lying flat and then draw it outside those bounds. -->
                    <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding}"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <ContentPresenter.LayoutTransform>
                            <Transform RotationAngle="90"/>
                        </ContentPresenter.LayoutTransform>
                    </ContentPresenter>
                </DataTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="Pane[LabelRotation=Right]">
        <Setter Property="HeaderTemplate">
            <Setter.Value>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding}"
                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                        <ContentPresenter.LayoutTransform>
                            <Transform RotationAngle="90"/>
                        </ContentPresenter.LayoutTransform>
                    </ContentPresenter>
                </DataTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- A TOOL group is dressed differently from a document one, and the difference is not decoration: a tool has a
         HEADER carrying its name and the two things you do to a tool and never to a document - put it away and close it -
         while its tabs sit along the BOTTOM. A document has many siblings and no caption of its own; a tool has few and
         is part of the workspace rather than of the work.
         Close buttons and the stretched lone tab are BEHAVIOUR, not looks: they live on PaneGroup and DockingArea so a
         host changes them without touching a theme. -->

    <Style Selector="PaneGroup[Kind=Tool]">
        <Setter Property="TabStripPlacement" Value="Bottom"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="PaneGroup">
                    <!-- The panel's OWN edge. A tool group states it here because it replaces the tab control's template
                         wholesale and would otherwise have none - and the divider between two panels draws nothing, so
                         without this there is no line anywhere and the panels bleed into each other. -->
                    <!-- The frame is NOT drawn here - see the Frame overlay at the end of the grid. This border only
                         groups the panel's parts. -->
                    <Border x:Name="Chrome">
                    <!-- Rows AND columns, because the strip belongs on a different side in each state: along the BOTTOM
                         while docked, and in a narrow COLUMN on the edge once folded (the triggers below move it). The
                         middle cell is the body; the outer tracks are Auto, so the one the strip is not in costs
                         nothing. -->
                    <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
                        <!-- The header. Its buttons are PARTS, so the group wires them once and a restyle cannot lose
                             them silently: a missing part is a button that does nothing, which is worse than none.
                             A quiet band closed with a hairline - see the note at the top of this file. -->
                        <Border x:Name="PART_Header" Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                                Background="{ResourceReference SolidBackgroundFillColorTertiary}"
                                BorderBrush="{ResourceReference ControlStrokeColorDefault}"
                                BorderThickness="{ResourceReference DockHeaderUnderline}"
                                Padding="{ResourceReference DockHeaderPadding}">
                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
                                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                        Content="{TemplateBinding Title}"
                                        FontSize="{ResourceReference FontSizeCaption}"
                                        Foreground="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"/>
                                <!-- Square, so the two line up with each other and sit centred in the header without
                                     anyone counting paddings - but at this theme's size, not a finger's. -->
                                <Button x:Name="PART_AutoHideButton" Grid.Column="1"
                                        Width="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                        Height="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                        MinWidth="0" MinHeight="0" Padding="0"
                                        Classes="CaptionButton"
                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                    <!-- The same thumbtack the overlay window's caption uses: cap (5..9), flared
                                         shoulders (3..11), tapering neck (6..8), point at (7,12). Drawn, never typed -
                                         a glyph depends on a font having it, and the one this started as is missing
                                         from most UI fonts, so the button rendered empty. -->
                                    <Path x:Name="AutoHideGlyph"
                                            Data="M5,2 L9,2 L9,4 L11,6 L11,7 L8,7 L8,10 L7,12 L6,10 L6,7 L3,7 L3,6 L5,4 Z"
                                            Stroke="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
                                            StrokeThickness="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"
                                            Fill="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                </Button>
                                <Button x:Name="PART_CloseButton" Grid.Column="2"
                                        Width="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                        Height="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                        MinWidth="0" MinHeight="0" Padding="0"
                                        Classes="CaptionButton CloseButton"
                                        HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                    <Path Data="M0,0 L10,10 M10,0 L0,10"
                                            Stroke="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
                                            StrokeThickness="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"
                                            HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                </Button>
                            </Grid>
                        </Border>

                        <Border x:Name="Body" Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                                Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                ClipToBounds="True">
                            <!-- DockedContent, not SelectedContent: while the panel is revealed its body lives in the
                                 flyout, and one piece of content belongs to one tree. -->
                            <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_SelectedContentHost"
                                    Content="{TemplateBinding DockedContent}"
                                    ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding SelectedContentTemplate}"
                                    ContentTemplateSelector="{TemplateBinding SelectedContentTemplateSelector}"/>
                        </Border>

                        <!-- Tabs BELOW the content, which is where a tool window keeps them while it is docked. -->
                        <TabStripScroller x:Name="PART_TabStrip" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal"
                                Margin="{ResourceReference DockStripInset}">
                            <!-- The strip carries the SELECTION INDICATOR over the headers, exactly as a plain
                                 TabControl's does: the control places and slides it and it only needs to exist and be
                                 out of the way of the pointer.
                                 Auto both ways: a Grid with no definitions stretches to whatever it is offered, and this
                                 one sits in the Auto column of a folded strip - so it made the narrow column as wide as
                                 the body used to be. Sized to its content, the column is the width of a turned label. -->
                            <Grid x:Name="StripFill" RowDefinitions="Auto" ColumnDefinitions="Auto">
                                <ItemsPresenter x:Name="PART_ItemsPresenter"/>
                                <Rectangle x:Name="PART_SelectionIndicator"
                                        Fill="{TemplateBinding SelectionIndicatorBrush}"
                                        Width="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"
                                        Height="{TemplateBinding SelectionIndicatorThickness}"
                                        HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                        VerticalAlignment="Bottom"
                                        IsHitTestVisible="False"/>
                            </Grid>
                        </TabStripScroller>

                        <!-- The FLYOUT of a revealed panel. Only the panel's BODY travels here - its tabs stay on the
                             edge, because that is what a revealed panel is: a strip you are glancing behind. It is a
                             popup so that it draws OVER the neighbours instead of pushing them aside; the strip is what
                             it is placed against, so it opens exactly where the panel would have been. Its own caption,
                             because the docked one is not on screen in this state - and a flyout you cannot dock back is
                             a panel you can only look at.
                             Placed against the GROUP, not the tab strip inside it: the strip sits at whatever inset the
                             chrome gives it, and a flyout lined up with the strip carried that inset along. -->
                        <Popup x:Name="PART_RevealFlyout" Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1"
                                PlacementTarget="{Binding ElementName=Chrome}"
                                Placement="Top" KeepOpen="False">
                            <!-- The size lives on the CONTENT, never on the popup: a popup is an element of this tree
                                 whether it is open or not, and a 240-wide one sat in the strip's own cell and took that
                                 room out of the layout even while shut. -->
                            <Border x:Name="FlyoutRoot"
                                    Background="{ResourceReference SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}"
                                    BorderBrush="{ResourceReference ControlStrokeColorDefault}"
                                    BorderThickness="{ResourceReference StrokeThin}">
                                <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*">
                                    <!-- A PART, because this is the caption a revealed panel is dragged by: while the
                                         flyout is up it is the only caption the panel has on screen. -->
                                    <Border x:Name="PART_FlyoutHeader" Grid.Row="0"
                                            Background="{ResourceReference SolidBackgroundFillColorTertiary}"
                                            BorderBrush="{ResourceReference ControlStrokeColorDefault}"
                                            BorderThickness="{ResourceReference DockHeaderUnderline}"
                                            Padding="{ResourceReference DockHeaderPadding}">
                                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto,Auto">
                                            <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                    Content="{TemplateBinding Title}"
                                                    FontSize="{ResourceReference FontSizeCaption}"
                                                    Foreground="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"/>
                                            <Button x:Name="PART_FlyoutAutoHideButton" Grid.Column="1"
                                                    Width="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                                    Height="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                                    MinWidth="0" MinHeight="0" Padding="0"
                                                    Classes="CaptionButton"
                                                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                                                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                                <!-- Hollow: this panel is NOT docked - that is the whole reason it is a
                                                     flyout. Pressing it is what docks it, and the tack fills in. -->
                                                <Path Data="M5,2 L9,2 L9,4 L11,6 L11,7 L8,7 L8,10 L7,12 L6,10 L6,7 L3,7 L3,6 L5,4 Z"
                                                        Stroke="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
                                                        StrokeThickness="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"
                                                        Fill="Transparent"
                                                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                            </Button>
                                            <Button x:Name="PART_FlyoutCloseButton" Grid.Column="2"
                                                    Width="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                                    Height="{ResourceReference DockHeaderButtonSize}"
                                                    MinWidth="0" MinHeight="0" Padding="0"
                                                    Classes="CaptionButton CloseButton"
                                                    HorizontalContentAlignment="Center" VerticalContentAlignment="Center"
                                                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
                                                <Path Data="M0,0 L10,10 M10,0 L0,10"
                                                        Stroke="{ObservableResource TextFillColorSecondary}"
                                                        StrokeThickness="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"
                                                        HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"/>
                                            </Button>
                                        </Grid>
                                    </Border>

                                    <!-- The body itself. The DOCKED presenter is emptied while this is open (see the
                                         Revealed trigger): one piece of content belongs to one tree, and a presenter
                                         holds its child even with the body hidden, so the flyout would be handed an
                                         element that already has a parent. -->
                                    <Border Grid.Row="1" x:Name="FlyoutBody"
                                            Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                            Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                            ClipToBounds="True">
                                        <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_FlyoutContentHost"
                                                Content="{TemplateBinding FlyoutContent}"
                                                ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding SelectedContentTemplate}"
                                                ContentTemplateSelector="{TemplateBinding SelectedContentTemplateSelector}"/>
                                    </Border>
                                </Grid>
                            </Border>
                        </Popup>

                        <!-- The panel's frame, drawn LAST and over everything. Under the content it was eaten: the
                             caption's ground and the body's ground are opaque and reach the panel's edge, so at
                             fractional scaling their outer pixel lands on the frame's and covers it. Over the content
                             nothing can take it, and it costs the layout nothing because it is not in the flow: no
                             inset, so switching panels does not nudge what is inside them. -->
                        <Border x:Name="Frame" Grid.Row="0" Grid.RowSpan="3" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="3"
                                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                                BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"/>
                    </Grid>
                    </Border>
                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <!-- Folded away: the thumbtack is an OUTLINE. Docked it is SOLID - that filled/hollow pair is
                             how every editor shows the state of auto-hide, and it is the button telling you what it
                             did. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsFolded" Value="true">
                            <Setter TargetName="AutoHideGlyph" Property="Fill" Value="Transparent"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- Not on an EDGE: there is nowhere to fold to, so auto-hide has nothing it could do. A button
                             that is present and does nothing is worse than one that is not there - it says the panel
                             refused, when in fact nothing was ever asked of it. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="Edge" Value="None">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_AutoHideButton" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- The whole of a floating window: the window's own title bar names it and carries the system's
                             close button, so a caption here would repeat the name word for word with a thumbtack that
                             has no edge left to fold against. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsFloatingRoot" Value="true">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_Header" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- One tab in a window of its own is not a choice, so there is no strip to make it with: the
                             window's title bar already says what this is, and a row of one button only takes room from
                             the thing being looked at. Dock a second panel in and the strip is back. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="ShowTabStrip" Value="false">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- REVEALED: in the tree this panel is still just its strip - the same as put away - and its
                             body is shown in the flyout OVER the neighbours. Emptying the docked presenter is not
                             cosmetic: one piece of content belongs to one tree, and a presenter goes on holding its
                             child even when the body around it is hidden.
                             IsOpen is driven by the control (PaneGroup.SyncFold), not from here: a trigger has to be
                             UNDONE to close the popup, and undoing it left the property Unset. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="State" Value="Revealed">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_Header" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="Body" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="Frame" Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="StripFill" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference DockFoldedStripFill}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- Which way the flyout opens, and how big it is: OUT of the edge the strip is on, as far
                             across as the panel is worth docked (RevealExtent) and as long as the strip itself
                             (RevealLength). Stated per edge because "out of" is a different direction on each side.
                             Harmless while the panel is docked - the popup is shut. -->
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="Edge" Value="Left">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="Placement" Value="Relative"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="HorizontalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetX}"/><Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="VerticalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetY}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealExtent}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Height" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealLength}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="Edge" Value="Right">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="Placement" Value="Relative"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="HorizontalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetX}"/><Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="VerticalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetY}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealExtent}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Height" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealLength}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="Edge" Value="Top">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="Placement" Value="Relative"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="HorizontalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetX}"/><Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="VerticalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetY}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Height" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealExtent}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealLength}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="Edge" Value="Bottom">
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="Placement" Value="Relative"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="HorizontalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetX}"/><Setter TargetName="PART_RevealFlyout" Property="VerticalOffset" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealOffsetY}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Height" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealExtent}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="FlyoutRoot" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding RevealLength}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <PropertyTrigger Property="State" Value="Collapsed">
                            <!-- Only a PUT-AWAY strip is a row of buttons rather than a strip you rearrange in: there is
                                 no panel open here to rearrange. A REVEALED panel IS open - its body is showing in the
                                 flyout - so its tabs reorder and tear off like any other. Keyed on Collapsed rather
                                 than on IsFolded, which covers both states and so switched dragging off for a panel
                                 that was open in front of the user. -->
                            <Setter Property="AllowTabDrag" Value="false"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_Header" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="Body" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
                            <!-- No frame around a put-away panel: a border encloses an AREA, and all that is left here
                                 is a strip of buttons on the edge. Framing it drew a box around nothing. -->
                            <Setter TargetName="Frame" Property="BorderThickness" Value="0"/>
                            <!-- The strip gets a fill of its own instead, which is what makes it READ as a put-away
                                 panel. Without it a folded panel is a row of labels floating on the background,
                                 indistinguishable from just some tabs - and the selection bar is gone too. -->
                            <Setter TargetName="StripFill" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference DockFoldedStripFill}"/>
                        </PropertyTrigger>
                        <!-- Folded against a SIDE, the tabs queue DOWN a narrow column on THAT edge - not along the
                             bottom band, which belongs to a docked panel. Moving the strip between cells is what keeps
                             it on the edge while a revealed body is showing beside it; the labels are turned by the
                             Pane styles above. -->
                        <MultiTrigger>
                            <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Condition Property="IsFolded" Value="true"/>
                                <Condition Property="Edge" Value="Left"/>
                            </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Orientation" Value="Vertical"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Grid.Row" Value="1"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Grid.Column" Value="0"/>
                            <!-- The bar turns with the strip: a stripe along the column, on the side facing the body.
                                 Its template geometry is the horizontal one, so the turned case states its own. -->
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding SelectionIndicatorThickness}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="Height" Value="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Right"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top"/>
                            <Setter Property="ItemsPanel">
                                <Setter.Value>
                                    <ItemsPanelTemplate><TabPanel Orientation="Vertical"/></ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                </Setter.Value>
                            </Setter>
                        </MultiTrigger>
                        <MultiTrigger>
                            <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                                <Condition Property="IsFolded" Value="true"/>
                                <Condition Property="Edge" Value="Right"/>
                            </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Orientation" Value="Vertical"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Grid.Row" Value="1"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_TabStrip" Property="Grid.Column" Value="2"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="Width" Value="{TemplateBinding SelectionIndicatorThickness}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="Height" Value="{ResourceReference StrokeThinValue}"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left"/>
                            <Setter TargetName="PART_SelectionIndicator" Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Top"/>
                            <Setter Property="ItemsPanel">
                                <Setter.Value>
                                    <ItemsPanelTemplate><TabPanel Orientation="Vertical"/></ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                </Setter.Value>
                            </Setter>
                        </MultiTrigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!-- The grip between two areas. INVISIBLE at rest, like every editor's. A drawn line here has nothing to say: the
         panels already have their own edges, and a stroke laid between two of them lands right next to those edges and
         reads as a double border, with a lump wherever three splitters meet. What the gap is for is GRABBING, and that
         is what Transparent gives - a hit-testable band with nothing drawn in it. -->
    <!-- A LINE down the middle of the gap, not a filled band. Lighting the whole divider put a solid bar where the
         layout's one piece of empty space is - the groove between two panels - and it read as a foreign object every
         time the pointer crossed it. This theme separates things with hairlines, so the grip answers with one: the
         BAND stays the full grab target (transparent, DividerThickness wide), and only a line inside it is drawn. -->
    <Style Selector="PaneSplitter">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="PaneSplitter">
                    <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}">
                        <!-- Default is a VERTICAL line: a splitter in a left-to-right host separates side by side, and
                             the host hands it its own orientation (see PaneHost). -->
                        <!-- One line per orientation, swapped by visibility rather than one line whose Width is
                             cleared: Width is a Double, so there is no value that means "back to Auto" for a trigger
                             to write, and the turned case kept the thickness it had instead of stretching.
                             Two units thick, not one: at a hairline the mark was there and nobody could see it. Half
                             the gap still reads as a LINE inside the groove rather than as the groove filled in. -->
                        <Grid>
                            <Border x:Name="LineV" Background="Transparent"
                                    Width="{ResourceReference StrokeAccentValue}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Stretch"/>
                            <Border x:Name="LineH" Background="Transparent"
                                    Height="{ResourceReference StrokeAccentValue}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Stretch" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                    Visibility="Collapsed"/>
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="PaneSplitter">
        <!-- Turned: a top-to-bottom host separates one above the other, so the line lies across. Width/Height both
             stated - the one that is not the thickness has to go back to Auto or it keeps the value it had. -->
        <PropertyTrigger Property="Orientation" Value="Vertical">
            <Setter TargetName="LineV" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed"/>
            <Setter TargetName="LineH" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible"/>
        </PropertyTrigger>
        <!-- Hover is ambient - crossing splitters is what a pointer does all day in a docked layout - so it answers in
             neutral. Both lines are painted; only one of them is on screen. -->
        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="true">
            <Setter TargetName="LineV" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference TextFillColorTertiary}"/>
            <Setter TargetName="LineH" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference TextFillColorTertiary}"/>
        </PropertyTrigger>
        <!-- The drag is a deliberate act and there is exactly one at a time, so this one keeps the accent. It also has
             to STAY lit: the pointer leaves the band the moment the boundary starts moving, so hover alone made the
             divider blink out exactly during the gesture it belongs to. -->
        <PropertyTrigger Property="IsDragging" Value="true">
            <Setter TargetName="LineV" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference AccentFillColorDefault}"/>
            <Setter TargetName="LineH" Property="Background" Value="{ResourceReference AccentFillColorDefault}"/>
        </PropertyTrigger>
    </Style>

    <!-- The compass shown while a pane is being dragged. Its brushes live here rather than in the control: which of the
         five targets is armed is said with COLOUR, and colour is the theme's business. -->
    <Style Selector="DockCompass">
        <Setter Property="IndicatorBrush" Value="{ResourceReference DockCompassFillColor}"/>
        <Setter Property="IndicatorStroke" Value="{ResourceReference DockCompassStrokeColor}"/>
        <Setter Property="ActiveBrush" Value="{ResourceReference AccentFillColorDefault}"/>
        <Setter Property="PreviewBrush" Value="{ResourceReference DockCompassPreviewColor}"/>
    </Style>

    <!-- Both overlays are WINDOWS, so they arrive with a window's look - a title bar, a border, a resize grip. An
         overlay has none of that: it is a plate that appears over everything for the length of a drag. This window
         covers the WHOLE docking area, so it must have nothing of its own: anything opaque here would black out the
         very layout the compass points at. -->
    <Style Selector="DockCompassWindow">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent"/>
        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DockCompassWindow">
                    <!-- The Content binding is NOT optional: without it the presenter presents nothing, the window lays
                         itself out and its content never does. Measured - the window arranged 8 times at the right size
                         while the compass inside it arranged zero. -->
                    <ContentPresenter x:Name="PART_ContentPresenter"
                            Content="{TemplateBinding Content}"/>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

</StyleSet>
